 ],
 "literal": "My lord, even eating simple gruel, has big shoulders! / We, even staying in the outer quarters, are like gold! / Opposing in war, if my lord enters the battlefield, / to the warriors who are bewildered and ruined in the festive place of the loud big town, / he is like a ghat that salt-merchants dread!",
 "translation": "My lord, though eating only simple gruel, has mighty shoulders! And we, though living in the outer quarters, are precious as gold! If he, opposing in war, enters the field of battle, to the warriors bewildered and ruined in the festive grounds of the loud, great town, he is like a deep river-crossing that salt-merchants dread!",
 "modern": "என் தலைவன் எளிய கஞ்சியை உண்டாலும் பெரிய தோள்களை உடையவன்; நாமோ புறச்சேரியில் இருந்தாலும் பொன்னைப் போன்றவர்கள்; போரை எதிர்த்து என் தலைவன் போர்க்களத்தில் புகுந்தால், ஒலியையுடைய பெரிய ஊரின் விழா நடக்கும் இடத்தில் மயங்கி அழிந்த வீரர்களுக்கு, உப்பு வணிகர்கள் அஞ்சும் நீர்த்துறை போன்றவன் ஆவான்.",
 "explanation": "This poem is set in the Kaikkiḷai tiṇai (one-sided love) and is addressed to the Chola king Porvaikko Perunarkilli by the poetess Nakkaṇṇaiyār. The speaker, a young woman, praises her hero's strength and her own devotion to him. She notes that even when eating simple gruel, her lord possesses mighty shoulders, and even when relegated to the outer quarters, she and her companions feel as precious as gold. When he enters the battlefield, he becomes a terrifying force to his enemies. The poet uses a vivid simile, comparing the hero to a dangerous river-crossing (tuṟai) that salt-merchants dread because their precious cargo would dissolve in the water. This image beautifully conveys the hero's formidable nature to his foes while highlighting his protective strength.",
